---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/math/intermediate/exponentials-and-logarithms">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Exponentials answer "what happens with constant <em>ratio</em> growth?"; logarithms are
    their undo button — <strong>the log is the exponent</strong>. If 2⁵ = 32, then log₂32 = 5.
    They are inverse functions, mirror images across the line y = x, and together they describe
    population growth, radioactive decay, earthquake sizes, pH and the decibel scale.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="each curve is the other reflected — growth up, growth down, one mirror between them" widget="plotter" config={inverseCfg} />

  <h2>The three log laws (all exponent rules in disguise)</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>log(ab) = log a + log b</strong> — multiplying numbers becomes adding logs. This single fact is why slide rules worked and why log-plots turn exponentials into straight lines.</li>
    <li><strong>log(a/b) = log a − log b.</strong></li>
    <li><strong>log(aⁿ) = n·log a</strong> — powers become coefficients; this is how you solve "how long until it doubles?" by bringing the exponent down.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Two results worth owning</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Doubling time:</strong> for growth rate r (compounded continuously), time to double
    is ln 2 / r ≈ 0.693/r — the honest version of the "rule of 70" from the economics lessons.
    <strong>Half-life:</strong> radioactive decay follows N = N₀·e^(−λt), and the log is the only
    way to untangle λ from a measured half-life — the method behind carbon-14 dating (half-life
    5,730 years).
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> OpenStax, Algebra and Trigonometry (Exponential and Logarithmic Functions).</p>
</TopicPage>
